WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:19.669
Pressing the 'magazine release' frees&nbsp;
the magazine from the lower receiver.

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:24.707
Rounds are loaded, and held under&nbsp;
high force of the magazine spring.

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:31.288
It's then inserted into the magwell&nbsp;
and secured by the 'magazine catch'.

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:36.649
Pulling and releasing the 'charging&nbsp;handle'
chambers the first round.

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:43.319
The charging handle forces the&nbsp;
'bolt carrier group' to the rear.

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:47.738
As it comes back, 
the 'action&nbsp;spring' is compressed.

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:51.440
As the carrier returns forward,

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:56.484
the 'bolt' strips the top cartridge from&nbsp;
the magazine and guides it into the chamber.

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:02.380
Housed within the bolt carrier, is the bolt.

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:09.012
The bolt contains several 'locking lugs'&nbsp;
that engage with the barrel extension lugs.

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:16.082
As it makes contact with the barrel breech&nbsp;face,
the bolt turns and locks into place.

00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:21.038
The locking function is due to the&nbsp;
'cam pin' housed within the bolt.

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:26.019
The pin rides along a 'track' in the&nbsp;
bolt carrier, allowing it to rotate.

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:30.320
When the bolt is open, it's&nbsp;
locked in the forward position&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:35.000
and can only rotate once the cam pin has&nbsp;
cleared the 'recess' in the upper receiver.

00:01:37.120 --> 00:01:42.694
In the rare occurrence where the bolt fails to&nbsp;
fully close, the 'forward assist' may be used.

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:45.600
Pressing the forward assist plunger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:49.920
allows the pawl to engage with the&nbsp;
notches cut into the bolt carrier.

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:54.484
This will force the carrier forward&nbsp;
until the bolt is fully locked.

00:01:55.840 --> 00:02:01.717
With the selector taken off the safe&nbsp;
position, the AR-15 is ready to fire.

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:06.758
A 'recess' cut into the selector&nbsp;
permits trigger movement.

00:02:08.099 --> 00:02:11.760
As the trigger is pulled, the&nbsp;
'hammer' is released from the sear.

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:16.160
The hammer spring drives the hammer&nbsp;
forward to strike the 'firing pin',&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.160 --> 00:02:20.484
causing it to travel forward&nbsp;
and impact the cartridge primer.

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:25.208
Within the primer 
is the 'ignition&nbsp;compound' and an 'anvil'.

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:29.600
As the firing pin strikes the&nbsp;
primer, a spark is created,&nbsp;

00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:32.358
igniting the propellant inside the cartridge.

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:36.960
The expanding gases propel&nbsp;
the bullet down the barrel,&nbsp;

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:41.583
where 'rifling grooves' impart&nbsp;
stabilizing spin on the bullet.

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:46.913
The gases escape through a small port&nbsp;
in the barrel.

00:02:47.375 --> 00:02:49.291
Through the gas tube...

00:02:49.932 --> 00:02:53.878
and into the 'bolt carrier&nbsp;
key', forcing it to the rear.

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:05.680
The 'extractor' grips the rim of the spent&nbsp;
cartridge case, and holds it against the bolt face&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:11.718
until the 'ejector' forces it through the ejection&nbsp;
port, striking the 'deflector' as it exits.

00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:21.360
As the bolt carrier comes back, it&nbsp;
returns the hammer to its cocked position,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:23.803
and stays held back by the 'disconnector'.

00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:28.480
When the 'buffer' reaches the&nbsp;
back of the receiver extension,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:31.840
the 'action spring' returns&nbsp;
the bolt carrier forward.

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:38.337
As it returns, the bolt strips a new cartridge&nbsp;
from the magazine and directs it into the chamber.

00:03:40.960 --> 00:03:45.691
Simultaneously, the extractor clips&nbsp;
into the rim of the new cartridge.

00:03:48.080 --> 00:03:52.000
Releasing the trigger releases&nbsp;
the hammer from the disconnector,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:58.512
and again the 'trigger sear' assumes control of&nbsp;
the hammer, readying the rifle for another shot.

00:04:00.329 --> 00:04:04.800
Military variants of the&nbsp;
AR-15, such as the M16 and M4,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:07.818
add a third option for fully automatic fire.

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:13.120
The inclusion of an 'auto sear', and&nbsp;
a hook on the back of the hammer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:16.588
allow continuous operation&nbsp;
while the trigger is depressed.

00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:22.080
Moving the selector to AUTO, the&nbsp;
disconnector is disabled from moving,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.080 --> 00:04:24.873
and will be unable to hold the hammer when fired.

00:04:25.360 --> 00:04:30.000
The auto sear is now able to rotate&nbsp;
into a recess in the selector.

00:04:31.840 --> 00:04:37.120
After a shot is taken, the auto sear rotates&nbsp;
forward as the bolt carrier comes back,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:39.200
taking control of the hammer.

00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:44.000
As the bolt carrier returns forward, the&nbsp;
back of the carrier trips the auto sear,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:47.057
releasing the hammer just after the bolt closes.

00:04:51.200 --> 00:04:56.532
Releasing the trigger, allows the sear&nbsp;
to again assume control of the hammer.

00:04:58.400 --> 00:05:02.160
In order to conserve ammunition&nbsp;
and promote greater accuracy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.160 --> 00:05:04.663
a burst mode was later introduced.

00:05:04.880 --> 00:05:09.482
With the auto sear still present,&nbsp;
the disconnector is split in two.

00:05:11.040 --> 00:05:15.148
The left disconnector takes control&nbsp;
of the hammer in semi-fire mode.

00:05:17.600 --> 00:05:21.368
Switching to BURST fire,&nbsp;
disables the left disconnector.

00:05:22.160 --> 00:05:28.372
The 'burst cam' and 'clutch spring' allow&nbsp;
the M4 to fire up to three rounds at a time.

00:05:28.880 --> 00:05:34.287
The clutch spring expands to rotate the&nbsp;
cam only when the hammer moves in reverse.

00:05:35.040 --> 00:05:41.158
As the trigger rotates forward, the burst&nbsp;
disconnector engages the deep notch of the cam.

00:05:41.440 --> 00:05:47.697
The cam rotates as the hammer is brought back, and&nbsp;
the disconnector engages the first small notch.

00:05:47.920 --> 00:05:51.455
This isn't enough for the disconnector to hold the&nbsp;hammer,&nbsp;

00:05:51.455 --> 00:05:54.653
and the auto sear is&nbsp;
tripped to fire another round.

00:05:54.960 --> 00:05:58.623
The cam rotates again and the cycle is repeated.

00:05:59.840 --> 00:06:05.000
The cam rotates once more, and the&nbsp;
hook engages the third, deeper notch.

00:06:05.440 --> 00:06:09.338
The disconnector is now able to&nbsp;
reach the hammer, holding it back.

00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:14.909
The trigger must be released and pulled&nbsp;
again to fire another three rounds.

00:06:22.960 --> 00:06:26.004
Once the final round of ammunition has been fired,&nbsp;

00:06:26.004 --> 00:06:31.288
the 'magazine follower' pushes up on the 'bolt&nbsp;catch', 
holding it open as it returns forward.

00:06:32.720 --> 00:06:37.638
Inserting a new magazine and pressing&nbsp;
the bolt catch, will chamber a new round.

00:06:41.040 --> 00:06:46.931
Using a cartridge, the front sight post&nbsp;
can be adjusted up or down for zeroing.

00:06:50.640 --> 00:06:53.600
The rear sight includes two 'apertures'.

00:06:53.600 --> 00:06:57.040
For short ranges, the zero&nbsp;
to two-hundred meter is used.

00:06:57.040 --> 00:07:00.253
For longer ranges, the smaller aperture is used.

00:07:00.800 --> 00:07:05.753
Turning the 'elevation knob' will raise and&nbsp;
lower the sight depending on the distance.

00:07:06.240 --> 00:07:10.080
The knob on the right of the sight&nbsp;
is used to adjust for 'windage'.

00:07:10.080 --> 00:07:15.840
The calibration lines on the back of the sight&nbsp;
help control windage adjustments during firing.

00:07:16.960 --> 00:07:20.240
The carry handle on the AR-15 can be removed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.240 --> 00:07:24.233
to allow for alternative optics,&nbsp;
such as holographic sights.

00:07:25.360 --> 00:07:29.259
Or longer range scopes, like the ACOG.

00:07:33.280 --> 00:07:36.560
Many ARs are equipped with an adjustable stock.

00:07:36.560 --> 00:07:42.131
The stock rides along the 'receiver extension',&nbsp;
commonly referred to as the buffer tube.

00:07:42.640 --> 00:07:44.160
Pressing the release lever,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.160 --> 00:07:48.752
moving it to the desired position and&nbsp;
releasing it, will lock it in place.

00:07:50.080 --> 00:07:54.000
The 'ejection port cover' can be&nbsp;
closed when the rifle is not in use,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.000 --> 00:07:57.347
to protect the inner parts from outside elements.

00:07:57.600 --> 00:08:01.576
The cover opens automatically&nbsp;
when the rifle is fired.

